# Blockquotes

@lede
A `>` followed by exactly one space. Subsequent matching lines continue
the quote. A blank line ends it.
@end

## The basic form

```brief
> An outer quote that spans
> two source lines.
```

Lines beginning with `> ` continue the same blockquote. The space after
`>` is required — `>text` is not a quote.

A live example:

> An outer quote that spans
> two source lines.

## Nesting

Nested blockquotes use additional `>` markers:

```brief
> An outer quote.
>> A nested quote inside.
> Back to the outer.
```

Live:

> An outer quote.
>> A nested quote inside.
> Back to the outer.

## Inline content

Inline emphasis, code spans, and shortcodes work inside a quote:

> A quote with *bold*, `code`, and @kbd[Ctrl+C] inside.

## When to reach for `@callout` instead

Blockquotes carry no semantic kind. If the content is a note, a tip, a
warning, or a caution — something the reader should treat with a specific
disposition — use `@callout` (with `kind: note | tip | important | warning |
caution`). See @ref[language/shortcodes.brf](Shortcodes).

## What ends a blockquote

A blank line, or any non-`>`-prefixed non-blank line. There is no
"continue with a leading space" rule the way some Markdown dialects allow.
